/* MODIFIED VERSION - ATTRIBUTION AND LICENSES ----------------------

  NODE_DRT by Samuel Drouin.

  This file is a MODIFIED version of OpenDRT v1.0.1 and is distributed
  under the GNU General Public License, version 3. NODE_DRT is the name of
  this product; OpenDRT is the name of the rendering engine it is built
  on, and belongs to its author. The two are not affiliated.

  ---- Rendering engine -------------------------------------------
  OpenDRT v1.0.1 by Jed Smith, licensed GPLv3.
  https://github.com/jedypod/open-display-transform
  The tonescale, rendering space, purity compression, brilliance and
  hue modules are his work. All credit for the image rendering itself
  belongs to him. He is not responsible for these changes.

  ---- Film look presets ------------------------------------------
  The seven look presets are sets of OpenDRT parameter values, fitted
  numerically against reference LUTs generated with spektrafilm.
  spektrafilm by Andrea Volpato, LUTs and profiles licensed CC BY-SA 4.0.
  https://github.com/andreavolpato/spektrafilm
  Each reference LUT is a spectral simulation of one negative stock on
  one print stock, built from that project's published profiles. No LUT
  data is embedded here: what remains are fitted parameter values, and
  they are a deliberate approximation - see NOTICE.txt for the measured
  deviation from each reference.

  ---- Modifications ----------------------------------------------
  Modified by Samuel Drouin, 22 September 2026:
    - original look presets replaced by seven fitted film looks
    - "Print Toning" module added: a per-level RGB tint driven by the
      tonescale, so shadows and highlights can pull in opposite directions
    - six trim controls added, all zero-default deltas on the selected
      look: Log Contrast, Pivot, Shadows, Highlight Purity, Brilliance
      and Mid Purity. Log Contrast is a pivoted S-curve applied ahead of
      the render, on the DaVinci Intermediate log signal, with an
      absolute pivot: 0.336 is 18 percent grey, and grey holds while
      contrast turns.
    - "Look Intensity" and "Toning Intensity" sliders, "Bypass Look"
      and "Bypass Toning" checkboxes
    - input gain per look, so switching a look no longer lifts mid grey
      and camera exposure stays judgeable
    - tonescale overlay carried per channel and drawn over the image
      rather than added to it, so R, G and B stay legible. The overlay
      now follows the same path as the image - one achromatic value
      through toe, toning and Shadows, tint reapplied last - which the
      original did per channel; and a "Curve Size" control shrinks the
      plot into the bottom-left corner
    - Clamp, HDR Grey and HDR Purity hardwired to their defaults
    - interface labels shortened, overlay lines thinned


  Modified by Samuel Drouin, 9 October 2026 (looks):
    - the seven film looks refitted against their spektrafilm references
      with the current engine: tone curve, colour and per-hue values solved
      together (least squares on 3727 colours, neutrals and skin weighted),
      under hard constraints - 18% grey held exactly, no gradient allowed to
      step back on neutrals, on a 64-hue chart or on neon ramps. Skin error
      to the reference is halved or better, neutral error divided by 2 to 3;
      highlights now open up as the prints do instead of sitting flat
    - neon artefacts removed: the fitted print-toning curves dipped in the
      last stop before white, and on saturated colours the dominant channel
      stepped back as light rose. The top 1.5 stops of each toning curve has
      a slope-limited twin, used in full on saturated colours and half on
      the rest; white stays neutral
    - grey anchored for every look with every Tonescale preset (table
      solved per preset and look), and the Default half of a Look Intensity
      mix now keeps the chosen Tonescale preset

  Modified by Samuel Drouin, 9 October 2026 (controls):
    - Look, Toning and Tone grouped as one section in the Inspector
    - a Skin section: Skin Line (skin hue pulled onto the vectorscope skin
      tone line, luminance held exactly), Skin Saturation (subtractive
      saturation model by Nikita Petrovich, on skin only), and light on the skin in three zones - Highlights, Midtones,
      Shadows - each an exposure in stops on scene light, before the curve,
      monotone whatever the settings, with Zone Pivot to place the zones
      on the face and Show Zones to see them
    - Tonescale Intensity: doses the Tonescale menu against the look's
      own curve by mixing the OUTPUT of the two curves, so the slider is
      linear and never steps back

  ---- Your rights ------------------------------------------------
  This file stays licensed under GPLv3. You may use, study, modify and
  redistribute it, including commercially. If you redistribute it, in
  any form and whether or not you charge for it, you must pass on these
  same freedoms: keep it GPLv3, keep this notice, and make the source
  available to whoever receives it.

  ---- Trademarks -------------------------------------------------
  Film and paper product names appearing in the documentation are
  trademarks of their respective owners and are used descriptively
  only, to say which photographic materials each preset was modelled
  on. This is an independent product, not affiliated with, sponsored
  by or endorsed by any film manufacturer. See TRADEMARKS.txt.

  Full license texts ship alongside this file: LICENSE-GPLv3.txt and
  SPEKTRAFILM_LICENSE.txt. See NOTICE.txt for the complete attribution.
  -------------------------------------------------*/



// Input settings
DEFINE_UI_PARAMS(in_gamut, 🎥 Gamut, DCTLUI_COMBO_BOX, 15, {i_xyz, i_ap0, i_ap1, i_p3d65, i_rec2020, i_rec709, i_awg3, i_awg4, i_rwg, i_sgamut3, i_sgamut3cine, i_vgamut, i_bmdwg, i_egamut, i_egamut2, i_davinciwg}, {XYZ, ACES 2065-1, ACEScg, P3D65, Rec.2020, Rec.709, Arri Wide Gamut 3, Arri Wide Gamut 4, Red Wide Gamut RGB, Sony SGamut3, Sony SGamut3Cine, Panasonic V-Gamut, Blackmagic Wide Gamut, Filmlight E-Gamut, Filmlight E-Gamut2, DaVinci Wide Gamut})
DEFINE_UI_PARAMS(in_oetf, 🎥 Log Curve, DCTLUI_COMBO_BOX, 1, {ioetf_linear, ioetf_davinci_intermediate, ioetf_filmlight_tlog, ioetf_acescct, ioetf_arri_logc3, ioetf_arri_logc4, ioetf_redlog3g10, ioetf_panasonic_vlog, ioetf_sony_slog3, ioetf_fuji_flog}, {Linear, Davinci Intermediate, Filmlight T-Log, ACEScct, Arri LogC3, Arri LogC4, RedLog3G10, Panasonic V-Log, Sony S-Log3, Fuji F-Log2})

// Tonescale Parameters
DEFINE_UI_PARAMS(tn_Lp, ☀ Peak nits, DCTLUI_SLIDER_FLOAT, 100.0, 100.0, 1000.0, 1.0)
// Les trois reglages d'OpenDRT qui avaient ete cables en dur a leurs valeurs
// par defaut. Rendus a l'utilisateur, avec ces memes valeurs par defaut : un
// projet existant rend donc exactement pareil qu'avant.
DEFINE_UI_PARAMS(tn_gb, ☀ HDR_Grey_Boost, DCTLUI_SLIDER_FLOAT, 0.13, 0.0, 1.0, 0.001)
DEFINE_UI_TOOLTIP(tn_gb, "How much middle grey is lifted as the display peak rises above 100 nits. It does nothing at 100 nits, which is why it only matters on an HDR display: there, holding grey where SDR puts it leaves the picture looking dim against the headroom above it. 0.13 is OpenDRT's own value.")
DEFINE_UI_PARAMS(pt_hdr, ☀ HDR_Purity, DCTLUI_SLIDER_FLOAT, 0.5, 0.0, 1.0, 0.001)
DEFINE_UI_TOOLTIP(pt_hdr, "How much of the extra headroom on an HDR display is spent holding colour rather than lightness. Like HDR Grey Boost it does nothing at 100 nits. Higher keeps saturated highlights coloured further up; lower lets them give way to white as they do in SDR.")
DEFINE_UI_PARAMS(out_clamp, ✂ Clamp_To_Display, DCTLUI_CHECK_BOX, 1)
DEFINE_UI_TOOLTIP(out_clamp, "Clips the result into the range the display can actually show. Leave it ON for anything you are looking at or delivering. Turn it OFF only to pass the render on to something downstream that wants the values that fell outside - a grade, a comp, an export in a wider space - because what leaves here will then contain negatives and values above one.")

// Clamp

DEFINE_UI_PARAMS(crv_enable, 📈 Curve View, DCTLUI_CHECK_BOX, 0)
DEFINE_UI_PARAMS(crv_size, 📐 Curve Size, DCTLUI_SLIDER_FLOAT, 1.0, 0.2, 1.0, 0.01)
DEFINE_UI_PARAMS(fc_overlay, 🌈 False_Color, DCTLUI_CHECK_BOX, 0)
DEFINE_UI_TOOLTIP(fc_overlay, "Exposure in stops from 18 percent grey - the Exposure (Stops) overlay of the Color Analyzer, identical: one colour per stop, thirteen bands from -6 and below to +6 and above, grey at mid grey. It is read the way the Color Analyzer reads it, on the luma of the INCOMING code values decoded by the Log Curve set in Input - so it tells you what the camera gave you, not what the look did with it, and on a DaVinci Intermediate timeline both tools paint the same picture identically. The legend down the left shows every band with its own colour, computed by the same function as the image. Turn it OFF before you render.")
DEFINE_UI_PARAMS(skin_check, 🧑 Skin_Check, DCTLUI_CHECK_BOX, 0)
DEFINE_UI_PARAMS(legend, 📋 Legend, DCTLUI_CHECK_BOX, 1)
DEFINE_UI_TOOLTIP(legend, "Draws the legend of whichever scope is on - the exposure zones for False Color, the four steps of the scale for Skin Check, the three light zones and their limits for Show Zones. One switch for both: they are never both on, and having to remember which checkbox belonged to which scope was the sort of thing you only discover while hunting for it. Turn it off once you know the scale and want the whole frame back.")
DEFINE_UI_TOOLTIP(skin_check, "Paints skin by how far it sits off the vectorscope's skin tone line, and reads exactly like the scope: YELLOW means on the line, GREEN that the tone has gone too green or too cool, MAGENTA too magenta or too warm, and the stronger the paint the further off it is. The face keeps its modelling under the paint, so you can see WHICH part of it is drifting instead of a flat sticker. Only colours that could plausibly be skin are painted - too little chroma, too much chroma or too little light and the pixel is left alone, so a red dress or a bowl of fruit does not come up as a face. The bar along the bottom is the same scale as the paint, with a white tick on the line. Turn it OFF before you render.")





